  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA ridge of high pressure is expected to maintain dry weather for\nmuch of the area through Thursday. The exception is Wednesday\nnight, where a weak crossing shortwave and warm advection could\nresult in a few showers north of Pittsburgh. The ridge should\nstrengthen again on Thursday ahead of an approaching trough,\nwith dry weather returning. Thursday will likely be the warmest\nday of the week, with highs around 80 outside of the higher\nterrain.\n\nA shortwave trough, and an associated surface cold front, is \nexpected to advance eastward from the Midwest on Thursday, \napproaching and crossing the Upper Ohio Valley region on Friday.\nThe upper trough is progged to weaken as it moves eastward\nacross our region on Friday. Increasing flow aloft will result\nin increasing shear during the day, with the area under the\nright entrance region to an upper level jet. Instability is\nprogged to remain minimal at this time, with SB CAPE around 250\nj/kg or less, mainly south of PIT. ML guidance indicates little\npotential for severe storms as well. The most favorable timing\nfor FROPA is early afternoon north of PIT, with mid to late\nafternoon for points southward. At this time, probabilities for\nat least a quarter of an inch of rain range from 60 to 80\npercent, though are less than 50 percent for a half an inch. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBroad upper troughing is progged to set up across the NE CONUS\nthrough the upcoming weekend. Saturday should be mainly dry and\ncooler as surface high pressure builds in from the north, though\na crossing southern stream wave should result in shower chances\nmainly south of I-70. Shower chances then increase on Sunday as\na northern stream shortwave rotates through the broad upper\ntrough. Cooler temperatures are expected, with highs just below\nseasonable levels.",
